WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.935 --> 00:00:19.957
Welcome to Geneva, Switzerland! I am Jean-Paul Baptiste, a watchmaker whose family moved here from France about a century ago.

2
00:00:19.958 --> 00:00:26.903
My grandfather, who was a watchmaker too, used to say to me, "Jean-Paul, all the best watchmakers are French."

3
00:00:26.904 --> 00:00:36.488
But now, in 1676, many French watchmakers live in Geneva; in fact, Geneva has the most watchmakers in the world.

4
00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:42.500
We are called cabinotiers because we work in shops almost as small as a cabinet.

5
00:00:42.501 --> 00:00:48.304
But even though our workshops may be small, the workmanship of our watches is grand indeed.

6
00:00:48.305 --> 00:00:55.020
Becoming a watchmaker takes hard work and patience because the parts of a watch are so tiny.

7
00:00:55.021 --> 00:01:03.049
The precision needed to create or repair a watch takes a long time to learn, so you must start as an apprentice.

8
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:09.962
For example, when I was 15, I apprenticed to an excellent watchmaker who is a friend of my grandfather.

9
00:01:09.963 --> 00:01:19.160
After five years as an apprentice a person with real aptitude will be hired by a master watchmaker to be an assistant in his workshop.

10
00:01:19.161 --> 00:01:26.061
The work is tedious at times, the hours are long, and the pay is meager, but you learn a lot.

11
00:01:26.062 --> 00:01:35.683
After my apprenticeship I was fortunate to be hired by Monsieur Girard, a master watchmaker and one of the best in Geneva.

12
00:01:35.684 --> 00:01:44.746
The rules of a watchmaker's education are set down by the Watchmakers' Guild of Geneva, which was established in 1601.

13
00:01:44.747 --> 00:01:53.985
The guild is a group that was created by talented watchmakers to ensure that every watchmaker in the city is an artist of the highest quality.

14
00:01:53.986 --> 00:01:59.817
In order to join the guild, a young man must work with a master watchmaker for three years,

15
00:01:59.818 --> 00:02:04.445
and within that time, he must create a watch to present to the guild.

16
00:02:04.446 --> 00:02:14.236
This watch is known as his masterpiece. If the guild approves his watch, the young man becomes a master and a member of the guild.

17
00:02:14.237 --> 00:02:18.012
Only then is he allowed to open his own shop.

18
00:02:18.013 --> 00:02:27.020
If the young watchmaker's watch is not approved, he can never open his own business and must toil as a watchmaker's assistant forevermore.

19
00:02:27.021 --> 00:02:33.242
As for me I successfully produced my masterpiece in just over a year.

20
00:02:33.243 --> 00:02:39.552
Naturally that watch is on display at my own workshop here on Saint-Gervais Street.

21
00:02:39.553 --> 00:02:43.232
Watchmaking requires many tools and accessories.

22
00:02:43.233 --> 00:02:51.841
First of all, there's my workbench, which is very important because it holds small hammers, brushes, tweezers,

23
00:02:51.842 --> 00:02:55.601
and anything else I need to repair or make a watch.

24
00:02:55.602 --> 00:02:59.255
This bench keeps my tools organized in small drawers,

25
00:02:59.256 --> 00:03:06.715
and I, like other watchmakers, have placed it in front of a large window that gets good natural light.

26
00:03:06.716 --> 00:03:15.548
Second, a watchmaker needs a magnifying eyeglass called a loupe to see close up the details of the inside of a watch.

27
00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:23.975
Third, various types of polishing and shaping implements are needed, such as oilstones and bluestones.

28
00:03:23.976 --> 00:03:29.951
Lastly, every watchmaker needs a small lathe, a very ancient tool.

29
00:03:29.952 --> 00:03:38.239
The lathe is a machine that holds a piece of material, such as wood or metal, in place and rotates it.

30
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:44.505
This allows a craftsman to shape the material using both hands, as he likes.

31
00:03:44.506 --> 00:03:53.992
I have operated my own shop for almost twenty years, and currently I have two apprentices, Max and Julian, who help keep the workshop clean.

32
00:03:53.993 --> 00:04:03.314
We do a lot of filing and shaping of metal, which creates countless tiny slivers of brass and steel that get all over the place.

33
00:04:03.315 --> 00:04:13.780
We also use large amounts of sperm whale oil to make sure the watch parts move together smoothly, but it drips on everything and makes a mess.

34
00:04:13.781 --> 00:04:18.288
Watchmaking has changed substantially since my grandfather's day.

35
00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:22.434
Back then a fusee was commonly used in watches.

36
00:04:22.435 --> 00:04:28.568
A fusee is a kind of cone-shaped device that has a post attached to a wheel.

37
00:04:28.569 --> 00:04:38.359
A chain is wrapped around the fusee, and the chain is attached to a metal coil, which is around another post inside a barrel-shaped container.

38
00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:44.219
The metal coil turns the barrel, and then the barrel turns the fusee by pulling on the chain.

39
00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:49.451
The fusee moves the wheel, and time ticks by as the metal coil slowly unwraps.

40
00:04:49.452 --> 00:04:55.454
When the metal coil completely uncurls, it can be wound up again by a watch key.

41
00:04:55.455 --> 00:05:03.295
This older type of watch had only an hour hand, and it didn't keep time very well since it kept running down quickly.

42
00:05:03.296 --> 00:05:08.009
Plus these watches were also thick and heavy.

43
00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:16.077
People wore them on chains around their necks as jewelry, and the wealthy decorated them with precious gems and gold.

44
00:05:16.078 --> 00:05:21.669
But this custom changed when the preacher John Calvin came to Geneva in the mid-1500s

45
00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:27.117
and passed laws that forbade anyone from wearing big pieces of jewelry.

46
00:05:27.118 --> 00:05:35.312
The watchmakers quickly realized they'd have to make their watches smaller and lighter, and they also wanted them to keep better time.

47
00:05:35.313 --> 00:05:42.038
After many years of experimentation, last year the spiral balance spring was invented.

48
00:05:42.039 --> 00:05:48.435
It is a finely coiled spring made of steel that balances the movements of the watch.

49
00:05:48.436 --> 00:05:54.906
Imagine a grandfather clock with a pendulum moving back and forth at an even speed.

50
00:05:54.907 --> 00:05:58.124
This movement drives the ticking of the clock.

51
00:05:58.125 --> 00:06:04.252
This new little spring does the same thing inside a watch.

52
00:06:04.253 --> 00:06:14.076
Timekeeping has now become more accurate, and watches made now have two hands instead of one—another hand has been added to count the minutes.

53
00:06:14.077 --> 00:06:19.099
Just think how our concept of time has changed because of this little invention.

54
00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:24.996
Now I can tell precisely how late my apprentices are arriving at work in the morning!

55
00:06:24.997 --> 00:06:33.775
It has certainly been an exciting year for watchmakers; my workshop has a large number of orders for watches with the spiral balance spring.

56
00:06:33.776 --> 00:06:38.857
We will be quite busy, I can assure you, but we won't just be making watches.

57
00:06:38.858 --> 00:06:43.830
My shop also makes surgical instruments and mechanical figures called automata.

58
00:06:43.831 --> 00:06:50.608
But I must admit, for me, making a beautiful pocket watch is the most rewarding work of all.

59
00:06:50.609 --> 00:07:00.636
Even though I am now 41 years old and my eyesight is not as good as it once was, I can still make masterpieces that will last a long time.

60
00:07:00.637 --> 00:07:05.605
Combining beauty and quality is the key to making a good timepiece,

61
00:07:05.606 --> 00:07:13.870
and Jean-Paul Baptiste has always been known on Saint-Gervais Street as a true artist!
